///1SYSVIEW
SYSVIEW.HLP - VT100/ANSI SYSTEM OVERVIEW FOR CP/M-80

Select a topic below. Press Return to move up one level.
From CP/M: HELP SYSVIEW

SYSVIEW displays a compact emulator overview and waits for a key.
It uses the CP/M console (VT100/ANSI) and the RETRO80-SYSINFO/1
emulator information interface.

Start: SYSVIEW
Refresh: R
Exit to CP/M: Q or Esc

///2STARTING
START SYSVIEW

Copy SYSVIEW.COM and this SYSVIEW.HLP to your CP/M-80 disk; the
RETRO80 system disk already carries both on A:.

At the CP/M prompt:
  SYSVIEW

Use a VT100/ANSI terminal with at least 80 columns and 24 rows.
The program clears the terminal and displays the overview.
There are no command-line options or configuration files.

To read this help:
  HELP SYSVIEW

SYSVIEW.COM is a CP/M-80 transient program, not a CP/M-86 CMD.

///2KEYS
REFRESH AND EXIT

  R or r       Rebuild the host snapshot and redraw the screen.
  Q or q       Exit SYSVIEW and return to CP/M.
  Esc          Exit SYSVIEW and return to CP/M.

No Return is needed after a command key.
Other ordinary keys do not change the displayed values.
Cursor keys are not required; some send sequences beginning with Esc
and may therefore cause the program to exit.

Exit restores normal text attributes and makes the cursor visible.
The screen is not continuously refreshed. Use R for a new snapshot.

///2DISPLAY
READ THE OVERVIEW

Fields shown by SYSVIEW itself (through the CP/M BDOS):
  CP/M BDOS version    Version word returned by BDOS function 12.
  Current CP/M drive   Drive letter returned by BDOS function 25.
  User area            User number returned by BDOS function 32.

Fields shown by the host stream (RETRO80-SYSINFO/1):
  Firmware             Firmware version string of the retro host.
  CPU core             The z80 instruction emulator running the guest.
  Guest RAM            The 64 KiB CP/M-80 guest address space.
  Host PSRAM           PSRAM installed on the ESP32-S3 board.
  Host uptime          Seconds since the host booted.
  Guest Z80 T-states   T-states executed since the last guest boot.
  Console / LIST / READER   Virtual device behaviour.
  Mounted virtual disks     Size, mode and SD/LittleFS path per slot.

Hardware information is emulator information, not host Mac
information. Long paths wrap. Non-printable characters never reach
the terminal.

///2INTERFACE
RETRO80-SYSINFO/1

  IN  F8             A6h when the firmware supports the interface,
                     FFh on older firmware (no writes happen then).
  OUT F8,1           Build a fresh snapshot on the host.
  IN  F9             Next snapshot byte; 00h ends the stream.

The stream begins with the header "RETRO80-SYSINFO/1" followed by
CR LF. A wrong header prints an "Invalid or unsupported" message.
The guest never decodes CPU FLAGS or guesses BIOS data; every host
value is delivered explicitly by the firmware.
